<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All About the Weather – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Perfect Day!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Fill in blanks in sentences using a word bank related to a familiar context. Interactive: Write simple words supported with drawings to peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can fill in blanks in sentences about the weather using a word bank (e.g., selecting "rainy" or "sunny" to complete "Today is a ___ day."). Can write simple, weather-related words supported with drawings to communicate ideas (e.g., drawing a rain storm and writes, "rainy"). Can express simple weather-related ideas with guidance (e.g., "I like rainy weather.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows weather flashcards (sun, rain, cloud, wind) and asks, "What's the weather like?". Students respond chorally. The teacher then quickly writes the first letter of each weather word on the board (e.g., S for sun, R for rain). Students come to the board to complete the word, focusing on initial sounds and spelling for 2nd graders (e.g., 'unny', 'ainy', 'oudy', 'indy'). Reinforce /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy", and initial /w/ in "windy".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a simple sentence with a blank: "Today is a ____ day." and shows a picture of a sunny day. The teacher says, "Today is a sunny day." and slowly writes "sunny" in the blank, explaining, "I chose 'sunny' from our word bank." The teacher also models writing "I like sunny days." on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'sunny' about rain?" (No.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write the word in the empty space?" (Yes.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, simple illustrated weather chart for the week with a few short, incomplete sentences (e.g., "Monday is a ____ day.", "I like to ____ on a ____ day."). The chart includes small pictures representing sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy days. Below the chart, there is a word bank: <i>sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, play, wear, umbrella, coat, outside, inside</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing the weather and activities: "It's a sunny day, and I play outside." Responding to questions: "What's the weather like?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture on the chart (e.g., a sun) and asks, "What is the weather like?" Students point to the word "sunny" in the word bank.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads a sentence aloud, "Today is a sunny day. I like to play ____." Students circle the correct word ("outside") from a choice of two words on a projected slide.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Adjectives + nouns (e.g., "rainy day"), Present simple (e.g., "I like sunny days."), WH-questions (e.g., "What is the weather like?"). Vocabulary: sunny, rainy, warm, cold, cloudy, windy, play, like, wear, coat, hat, umbrella, boots, outside, inside.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Fill-in-the-Blanks (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with 3-4 simple sentences about the weather, each with a blank and an accompanying drawing. A word bank (e.g., <i>sunny, rainy, windy, cloudy, coat, umbrella</i>) is provided. For example: "1. Today is a ____ day. (drawing of sun)" or "2. It's ____. I need my ____. (drawing of rain and umbrella)". Students choose the correct words from the bank and *write* them in the blanks. The teacher walks around, providing support and checking spelling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Sentence Expansion (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> The teacher writes a basic sentence on the board, "It is a ____ day." and models adding a preference: "It is a sunny day, and I like to play outside." Then students get a new worksheet with sentences like: "It is a ____ day." and a picture (e.g., a cloudy sky). They choose a weather word from a bank to fill the first blank and then *write* a simple activity or clothing item for the second part, using "and". (e.g., "It is a cloudy day, and I wear my jacket."). They practice writing simple ideas with "and."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>"Draw and Write" Peer Exchange (Interactive Writing):</b> In pairs, Student A draws a simple picture of a weather type (e.g., a storm or a rainbow) on a small card and *writes* one simple weather word or phrase underneath (e.g., "stormy," "rainy day"). Student B receives the card, looks at the drawing, and checks if the word matches the drawing and is written clearly. Then they switch roles. This activity reinforces phonemic awareness by relating the written word to a concept (e.g., /w/ in 'windy', final /i/ in 'windy').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About the Weather"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will create a "My Perfect Day!" weather card. Each student receives a blank card or a template with a box for drawing and two lines for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students think about their "perfect day" weather.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw a picture representing their perfect weather scenario (e.g., sun, snow, clouds).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below the drawing, they *write* two simple sentences using the target grammar and vocabulary. Example sentences modelled by teacher: "My perfect day is ____. I like to ____." or "It is a ____ day. I wear my ____." They are encouraged to use adjectives + nouns (e.g., "sunny day") and express preferences ("I like sunny days.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can share their card with a partner or display it on a "Perfect Day" wall.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The "My Perfect Day!" weather card created in Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation/Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Fills in blanks in sentences about the weather using a word bank. (from previous practice stages)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes simple, weather-related words supported with drawings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses adjectives + nouns (e.g., "sunny day").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expresses a simple preference (e.g., "I like sunny weather.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of written words for 2nd grade.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two quick questions by circling 'Yes' or 'No' on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write weather words today?" (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can my friend read my weather sentences?" (Yes / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite weather word to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn to write about your perfect day?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What writing tasks worked best for 2nd graders to fill in blanks and write simple sentences? Were the word banks appropriately scaffolded? What specific words or sentence structures need more practice? How can I further integrate phonemic awareness for spelling (e.g., /w/ and /i/ in "windy") in writing activities? What adjustments are needed for Lesson 5, where students will integrate their learning into a final project?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5, students will use their "My Perfect Day!" cards and other weather descriptions to create a collaborative "Class Weather Book" and share their ideas, consolidating their listening, reading, speaking, and writing skills about the weather. This will involve summarizing information and presenting their work.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>